# TypeScript Guidelines

**General TypeScript coding standards and function declaration patterns.**

## **Function Declarations**

- **Use arrow functions over function declarations** throughout the codebase
- **Apply to all function types** - utilities, event handlers, helpers

```ts
// ✅ DO: Arrow functions
const handleClick = () => {
  /* logic */
}

const processData = (data: string[]) => data.map((item) => item.trim())

const calculateTotal = (items: number[]) =>
  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item, 0)

// ❌ DON'T: Function declarations
function handleClick() {
  /* logic */
}

function processData(data: string[]) {
  return data.map((item) => item.trim())
}

function calculateTotal(items: number[]) {
  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item, 0)
}
```

## **Type Definitions**

- **Use clear, descriptive type names**
- **Prefer type over interface** for simple object shapes
- **Use interface for extensible object definitions**

```ts
// ✅ DO: Clear type definitions
type UserRole = 'admin' | 'user' | 'moderator'

type ApiResponse<T> = {
  data: T
  status: number
  message: string
}

interface User {
  id: string
  name: string
  email: string
  role: UserRole
}

// ❌ DON'T: Unclear or inconsistent typing
type U = { n: string; e: string }

interface Response {
  d: any
  s: number
}
```

## **Early Return Pattern (Guard Clauses)**

- **Use early returns for validation and error cases** - Prefer guard clauses over nested if statements
- **Return early for edge cases** - Reduces nesting and improves readability
- **Keep the main logic at the top level** - Makes the "happy path" more obvious

```ts
// ✅ DO: Early returns (guard clauses)
const processUser = async (userId: string | null) => {
  if (!userId) {
    return { error: 'User ID is required' }
  }

  const user = await getUser(userId)

  if (!user) {
    return { error: 'User not found' }
  }

  // Main logic continues here without deep nesting
  return { data: user }
}

// ❌ DON'T: Nested ifs (arrow code/pyramid of doom)
const processUser = async (userId: string | null) => {
  if (userId) {
    const user = await getUser(userId)
    if (user) {
      // Main logic deeply nested
      return { data: user }
    } else {
      return { error: 'User not found' }
    }
  } else {
    return { error: 'User ID is required' }
  }
}
```

## **Error Handling**

- **Use arrow functions for error handlers**
- **Provide meaningful error messages**
- **Handle errors consistently**

```ts
// ✅ DO: Consistent error handling
const handleApiError = (error: Error) => {
  console.error('API Error:', error.message)
  return { success: false, message: error.message }
}

const safeParseJson = (jsonString: string) => {
  try {
    return { success: true, data: JSON.parse(jsonString) }
  } catch (error) {
    return handleApiError(error as Error)
  }
}

// ❌ DON'T: Inconsistent error handling
function handleError(e: any) {
  throw e
}

const parseJson = (str: string) => JSON.parse(str) // No error handling
```

**Follow these patterns for consistent, maintainable TypeScript code.**
